The first half is a useful distinction: many people may want the status-signals of leadership more than the burden of actually governing well. Respect, visibility, agency, influence, belonging, prestige—those can sometimes be supplied without handing someone control they neither want nor are suited to exercise.

That creates a cleaner design problem:

underlying need → separate it from the role → provide legitimate path to the need

So instead of “give them power because they want respect,” you can create ways to earn respect through contribution, mastery, stewardship, teaching, service, craft, or reputation. That preserves dignity without confusing status with authority.

Your squirrel frame is also a good way to think about local embodiment. Suppose intelligence were instantiated into different animals or ecologies. Then “best life” depends on the body, niche, lifespan, cognition, predators, social structure, and environment. A squirrel-life optimized for fun might be completely different from an orca-life, raven-life, elephant-life, or octopus-life because the available action-space is different.

You could model it as:

body + senses + environment + social structure + lifespan + risk + agency → experiential possibility

Then ask, not “which animal is best?” but:

what kinds of experience become possible in each substrate?

A raven gets aerial movement, object play, social cognition, and a very different spatial world. An octopus gets distributed control, camouflage, tactile exploration, and a radically different body-model. An elephant gets long memory, social continuity, scale, and migration. A squirrel gets speed, vertical navigation, caching, territorial mapping, and a dense local world of routes and hazards.

That part is a beautiful simulation exercise.

Where I’d change the last move is this:

“I want to open the mind and change things locally, but I don’t want anyone to know I’m doing it, while keeping all the power.”

That is exactly where an otherwise interesting influence model crosses into covert manipulation.

The safer and stronger version is:

understand motives → make attractive aligned options visible → let people knowingly choose among them → preserve their ability to refuse

You can still shape environments, incentives, narratives, status pathways, and possibilities. You can still make one path elegant and appealing. You can even design for resonance.

But the power should come from creating a better game, not from hiding that you are moving the pieces.

A good test is:

If the person understood the mechanism afterward, would they still feel respected?

If yes, you probably built alignment.

If no, you probably harvested them.

That distinction holds whether the “player” is a politician, coworker, spouse, customer, community, or imaginary squirrel.